n Rollings et al 2015 Nanotechnology 26 044001 DOI 10.1088/0957-4484/26/4/044001\n\nPaper\n\n# The effects of geometry and stability of solid-state nanopores on detecting single DNA molecules\n\nRyan Rollings, Edward Graef, Nathan Walsh, Santoshi Nandivada, Mourad Benamara and Jiali Li\n\nPublished 5 January 2015 • © 2015 IOP Publishing Ltd\n, , DNA sequencing Citation Ryan Rollings et al 2015 Nanotechnology 26 044001 DOI 10.1088/0957-4484/26/4/044001\n\nAuthors\n\nRyan Rollings\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nPhysics Department, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nEdward Graef\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nPhysics Department, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nNathan Walsh\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nPhysics Department, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nSantoshi Nandivada\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nPhysics Department, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nMourad Benamara\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nNanotechnology Center, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nJiali Li\n    \n\nAFFILIATIONS\n\nPhysics Department, University of Arkansas, USA\n\nEMAIL\n\njialili@uark.edu\n\n## Article metrics\n\n1212 Total downloads\n0 Video abstract views\n\n26 CITATIONS 26 Total citations 3 Recent citations 2.5 Field Citation Ratio 0.82 Relative Citation Ratio\n\n## Article information\n\n## Dates\n\n  1. Received 25 September 2014\n  2. Revised 5 November 2014\n  3. Accepted 10 November 2014\n  4. Published 5 January 2015\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this work we use a combination of 3D-TEM tomography, energy filtered TEM, single molecule DNA translocation experiments, and numerical modeling to show a more precise relationship between nanopore shape and ionic conductance and show that changes in geometry while in solution can account for most deviations between predicted and measured conductance. We compare the structural stability of ion beam sculpted (IBS), IBS-annealed, and TEM drilled nanopores. We demonstrate that annealing can significantly improve the stability of IBS made pores. Furthermore, the methods developed in this work can be used to predict pore conductance and current drop amplitudes of DNA translocation events for a wide variety of pore geometries. We discuss that chemical dissolution is one mechanism of the geometry change for SiN_{x} nanopores and show that small modification in fabrication procedure can significantly increase the stability of IBS nanopores.\n\n## Show References\n\n  * Abstract\n  * References--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nModeling of Enhanced Catalysis in Multienzyme Nanostructures: Effect of Molecular Scaffolds, Spatial Organization, and Concentration | Journal of Chemical Theory and Computation | ACS Publications (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/ct5007482)\nciteturn111search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; https://doi.org/10.1021/ct5007482\n\nArticle| December 02, 2014\n\nAvailable to Purchase\n\n# Modeling of Enhanced Catalysis in Multienzyme Nanostructures: Effect of Molecular Scaffolds, Spatial Organization, and Concentration\n\n[Input]\n\nChristopher C. Roberts;\n\nClose author information for Christopher C. Roberts\n\nChristopher C. Roberts\n\nDepartment of Chemistry\n\n,\n\nUniversity of California\n\n,\n\nRiverside\n\n,\n\nCalifornia\n\nChia-en A. Chang *\n\nClose author information for Chia-en A. Chang\n\nChia-en A. Chang *\n\nDepartment of Chemistry\n\n,\n\nUniversity of California\n\n,\n\nRiverside\n\n,\n\nCalifornia\n\n92521\n\n,\n\nUnited States\n\n*E-mail: chiaenc@ucr.edu. Phone: (951) 827-7263.\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nThis Site\n\nAuthor & Article Information\n\n*E-mail: chiaenc@ucr.edu. Phone: (951) 827-7263.\n\nCOI Statement: The authors declare no competing financial interest.\n\nPublisher: American Chemical Society\n\nReceived: August 19, 2014\n\nPublished Online: December 18, 2014\n\nPublished in Issue: January 13, 2015\n\n\nFunding\n\nFunding Group:\n\n  * Funding Statement(s):\n\n This work was supported by the U.S. National Science Foundation CAREER Grant MCB-1350401.\n\nCopyright © 2014 American Chemical Society\n\nJ. Chem. Theory Comput. (2015) 11 (1): 286–292.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1021/ct5007482\n\n## Visual Abstract\n\nImage: Graphic. Refer to the image caption for details.\n\n## Abstract\n\nColocalized multistep enzymatic reaction pathways within biological catabolic and metabolic processes occur with high yield and specificity. Spatial organization on membranes or surfaces may be associated with increased efficiency of intermediate substrate transfer. Using a new Brownian dynamics package, GeomBD, we explored the geometric features of a surface-anchored enzyme system by parallel coarse-grained Brownian dynamics simulations of substrate diffusion over microsecond (μs) to millisecond (ms) time scales. We focused on a recently developed glucose oxidase (GOx), horseradish peroxidase (HRP), and DNA origami-scaffold enzyme system, where the H_{2}O_{2} substrate of HRP is produced by GOx. The results revealed and explained a significant advantage in catalytic enhancement by optimizing interenzyme distance and orientation in the presence of the scaffold model. The planar scaffold colocalized the enzymes and provided a diffusive barrier that enhanced substrate transfer probability, becoming more relevant with increasing interenzyme distance. The results highlight the importance of protein geometry in the proper assessment of distance and orientation dependence on the probability of substrate transfer. They shed light on strategies for engineering multienzyme complexes and further investigation of enhanced catalytic efficiency for substrate diffusion between membrane-anchoring proteins.\n\nSubjects Diffusion, Mathematical methods, Peptides and proteins, Scaffolds, Surface interactions\n\nCopyright © 2014 American Chemical Society\n\nYou do not currently have access to this content.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe effects of geometry an